From e495cf8975e7a2fbebb059ddb879c0d40a99a41d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 19:52:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] superseded/stale backend: release host-loop leases
 at once, start no new wakes

Live proof t_01a1d64edc10dd1e on hermes-hub (2026-11-13): the always-on backend
:9148 (booted on 17f28630e4, before the fix) took the kanban-unloaded lease at
19:10:13 while the gateway restarted on the fix, was superseded at 19:11:14, and
still started a sweep wake at 19:11:19 during its drain. The wake failed on the
pre-fix code (SubscriptionQuotaUnavailable, pre-fix traceback line numbers) and
booked its replay at the wall reset (Nov 17), so one stale wake deferred that
chat's notice by 4 days. The lease went to the gateway only at 19:11:55.

Five whys: the notice was deferred 4 days <- a failed wake booked its replay
(and the close-out deferral) at the quota wall's reset <- the wake ran on stale
code that hits a wall the fix on main routes around <- the superseded backend
still held kanban-unloaded and ran host passes during its drain <- the
superseded watchdog only drained HTTP connections; nothing told the host-loop
lease or the waker that this process is being replaced.

Fix:
- host_loop_lease: retire_host_loops(key, reason) / resume_host_loops(key) /
  host_loops_retired(). Retiring releases every lease of the process at once;
  held() and exclusive() stay False (kill switch included) while any reason is
  set, and a hand-off wait that wins the lock meanwhile gives it straight back.
- web_server_skew_exit: the superseded watchdog retires the host loops on the
  first supersede observation (resumes if the roll aborts). It and the code-skew
  watchdog also retire them after two consecutive skew reads (boot commit
  behind the checkout; StaleCodeLoops, read every 10 s on the 2 s superseded
  poll), so a stale always-on backend never takes the lease while the gateway
  restarts. Only the open connections drain.
- kanban_unloaded.run_pass: a retired process runs neither the host nor the
  residual pass ("retired").
- _wake_session_turn refuses before resume/build in a retired process ("busy",
  which every caller already defers to a later pass that the successor runs).
- automation_replay.trusted_resets_at: a build failure's failure_resets_at is
  ignored when the failing process runs stale code (retired, or code skew), so
  the replay and notice_guarantee's close-out deferral take the short delay.
  _replay_failed_wake moves to automation_replay.replay_failed_wake (offsets
  session_notifications' FILE_LINES cap).

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_superseded_host_loops.py (12): release on
supersede while draining with a busy fence, aborted roll resumes, skew before
supersede releases, single skew blip keeps, code-skew watchdog releases,
run_pass "retired", wake refused before resume, stale-code replay/close-out
use the short delay while current code still waits for the reset. RED on main
(API absent); 45 related files green.

Reverse: revert this commit.
